s="\"The triangle-free Hedetniemi conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/344593/the-triangle-free-hedetniemi-conjecture)\\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The triangle-free Hedetniemi conjecture ... This is presented as a weaker version of Hedetniemi's conjecture, motivated by the fact that the known counterexamples contain large cliques.\\n\\nThe triangle-free Hedetniemi conjecture\\n\\nLet $G$G and $H$H be finite undirected simple triangle-free graphs, meaning that neither graph contains a copy of $K_3$K 3​. Let $G\\\\times H$G×H denote their tensor product and let $\\\\chi(G)$χ(G) denote chromatic number. Triangle-free Hedetniemi conjecture.\\n\\n$$\\\\chi(G\\\\times H)=\\\\min\\\\{\\\\chi(G),\\\\chi(H)\\\\}.$$χ(G×H)=min{χ(G),χ(H)}.\\n\\nThis is presented as a weaker version of Hedetniemi's conjecture, motivated by the fact that the known counterexamples contain large cliques. The source does not provide a resolution of this triangle-free case.\\n\\nSources & references View supporting material\\n\\n## Primary source\\n\\nRyoya Fukasaku, Michitaka Furuya and Akihiro Higashitani, “An algebraic reduction of Hedetniemi's conjecture”, arXiv:1911.09799 (2019).\\n\\ngraph-coloringgraph-productstriangle-free-graphs\\n\\nAdd a comment 0\\n\\n## Progress summary\\n\\nRefresh Never refreshed\\n\\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\\n\\n## Solutions 0\\n\\nNo solutions have been posted yet.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nHedetniemi's conjecture is asymptotically false (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/am/pii/S0095895620300228)\\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; # Hedetniemi's conjecture is asymptotically false ... Also, G has girth at least 6 and in particular is triangle-free, so Lemma 5 applies to the graph {G}^{\\\\circ }.\\n\\n## Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B\\n\\nDate: January 2021\\n\\nPages: 485-494\\n\\nVolume: Volume 146\\n\\n## Published by: Elsevier\\n\\n\\n# Hedetniemi's conjecture is asymptotically false\\n\\nAuthor links open overlay panel Xiaoyu He ^{1}, Yuval Wigderson ^{1}\\n\\nShow more\\n\\nView PDFDownload full issueView Open Manuscript\\n\\nCite\\n\\nAdd to Mendeley\\n\\nShare\\n\\n10.1016/j.jctb.2020.03.003\\n\\nMore actions\\n\\n  * Article\\n  * Recommended articles\\n  * Cited by (8)\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nExtending a recent breakthrough of Shitov, we prove that the chromatic number of the tensor product of two graphs can be a constant factor smaller than the minimum chromatic number of the two graphs. More precisely, we prove that there exists an absolute constant $\\\\delta >0$ such that for all c sufficiently large, there exist graphs G and H with chromatic number at least $(1+\\\\delta )c$ for which $\\\\chi (G\\\\times H)\\\\le c$.\\n\\n## Keywords\\n\\nGraph coloring\\n\\n;\\n\\nHedetniemi's conjecture\\n\\n  * Previous article in this issue\\n\\n## 1. Introduction\\n\\nIf G and H are finite graphs, their tensor product is the graph on where vertices and are adjacent if and only if and ; here and throughout, we use the notation ∼ to denote adjacency. By composing with the projection maps to each coordinate, it is easy to check that the chromatic number satisfies(1) for all finite graphs G and H. In 1966, Hedetniemi [9] conjectured that equality always holds in (1). This conjecture has received a considerable amount of attention; for instance, it was proved if G and H are 4-colorable [3], if every vertex in G is contained in a large clique [1], or if G and H are Kneser graphs or hypergraphs [7]. Additionally, many natural variants of this conjecture have been studied. For instance, Hajnal [8] proved that the analogous conjecture is false for infinite graphs, while Zhu [16] proved that the analogous conjecture for fractional colorings is true. We refer the reader to the excellent surveys [11], [13], [15] for more information on work surrounding Hedetniemi's conjecture.\\n\\nIn a recent breakthrough, Shitov [12] disproved Hedetniemi's conjecture by demonstrating that for sufficiently large c, there exist graphs G and H with , but . Even more recently, Tardif and Zhu [14] proved that the gap between and can be arbitrarily large, i.e. that for every integer d and for all c sufficiently large, there exist graphs G and H with , but . They also raised the question of whether the gap d can be made to be linear in c, and proved that this is possible under the additional assumption that Stahl's conjecture on multichromatic numbers is true.\\n\\nIn this paper, we modify Shitov's construction to answer Tardif and Zhu's question in the affirmative, showing that the ratio of and is asymptotically bounded away from 1.\\n\\nTheorem 1\\n\\nThere is an absolute constant such that for all sufficiently large c, there exist simple graphs with , and .\\n\\nEquivalently, we may express Theorem 1 in terms of the Poljak–Rödl function [10], which is defined by Then Hedetniemi's conjecture is equivalent to the statement that for all k. The so-called weak version of Hedetniemi's conjecture simply asks whether , and is still open; however, Poljak and Rödl [10] proved that either or is bounded by 9. With this notation, Theorem 1 is equivalent to the statement that for all sufficiently large k. For comparison, Shitov [12] proved that for sufficiently large k, and Tardif and Zhu [14] proved that . Moreover, they proved that the stronger bound would follow from Stahl's conjecture.\\n\\nThe proof of Theorem 1 closely mirrors Shitov's proof of the main result in [12]. Roughly speaking, the main innovation in our argument is that whereas Shitov constructs one uncolorable vertex to prove , we construct a large clique \\\\mathcal{N} of δc uncolorable vertices of H (see Lemma 6) to obtain the stronger lower bound .\\n\\nFor the sake of clarity of presentation, we systematic